<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lezhë</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lissus, Alessio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lezhë is a town in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drin">Drin</a>. As ancient Lissus it was a fortified Illyrian and Greek settlement where Illyrian kings struck coins, and it later became a Roman town. In 1444 it hosted the assembly that formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a>, the alliance of Albanian nobles under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg</a>, who was buried in its cathedral in 1468.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lezhe.jpg" alt="Lezhë" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pasztilla aka Attila Terbócs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lezh%C3%AB,_Albania_%E2%80%93_Skanderbeg_Memorial_2016_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lezhë is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, seat of Lezhë County and Lezhë Municipality, and is one of the country's oldest continuously inhabited cities, with roughly 2,400 years of recorded history. A settlement with fortifications stood on a 413-metre mountain, the Mal i Shëlbuemit, from at least the 8th century BCE, near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drin">Drin</a> river. Swords found there are attributed to exchange with Mycenaean Greece in the early Mycenaean period. The later city was built on a lower hill of 172 metres, with ramparts facing the Drin valley and the coast, to guard the route inland and to give Illyrian ships a secure anchorage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest fortification walls of the proto-urban settlement are of typical Illyrian construction and date to the late 4th century BCE. The name Lissos is considered Greek, from a word meaning smooth rock. By the 3rd century BCE Lissus was one of the main cities of the Illyrian kingdom under the Ardiaean and Labeatan dynasties. In the peace treaty of 228 BCE the Illyrian queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teuta">Teuta</a> promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> not to sail south of Lissus with more than two unarmed ships. In 211 BCE Philip V of Macedon captured its citadel, Acrolissus, and the city became the Macedonian outlet to the Adriatic, though it was later recovered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a>.</p><p>Under the Illyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentius">Gentius</a>, in the first half of the 2nd century BCE, Lissus minted coins for the ruler, and it was from Lissus that he organised his army against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Coins show that it kept a large degree of municipal autonomy under both Macedonian and Illyrian rule. It was later subjected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and lay in the province of Epirus Nova. Slavic populations captured it in the early 590s, and Byzantine control was re-established in the early 9th century.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the town, known in Italian as Alessio, frequently changed masters until the Venetians took it in 1386. Because it was under Venetian control, Gjergj Kastrioti Skanderbeg chose it in 1444 as a neutral place for a convention of Albanian lords to organise their common defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a>. Skanderbeg was buried in the cathedral, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>. The town fell to the Turks in 1478, and the cathedral was later used as the Selimie Mosque.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Excavations begun in 2004 around the ancient acropolis of Lissos and the Skanderbeg Memorial have revealed Hellenistic, Roman and early Byzantine buildings and tombs. The city lies near the port of Shëngjin on the national road between the Montenegrin border and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lezhë” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Venetian patronage, Skanderbeg summoned Albanian noblemen to <b>Lezhë</b>, where they formed a military alliance against the Ottomans.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal coins were struck at Scodra and <b>Lissus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/illyrian-kingdom">Illyrian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>On 2 March 1444, in the Venetian-held town of <b>Lezhë</b>, Skanderbeg gathered the leading Albanian nobles, among them the Arianiti, Dukagjini, Muzaka, Thopia, Zaharia and Balšić families and Stefan Crnojević of Zeta.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-lezhe">League of Lezhë</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1387 they held the port of <b>Lezhë</b> and its hinterland, between the territories of the Balšić lords of Zeta and the Kastrioti.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-dukagjini">Principality of Dukagjini</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Gjon Kastrioti built a lordship stretching from the Mat valley towards the Adriatic coast near <b>Lezhë</b>, accepting Ottoman overlordship after the 1380s while trading with Venice and Ragusa.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kastrioti">Principality of Kastrioti</a></li></ul></section>
